  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 4: Gapminder                                             -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-gapminder">
    <title>Building Up Dimensions: The Gapminder Activity</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Hans Rosling's Gapminder data make the same point as the elephants, with
        a dataset you can actually hold in your hands.
        The data are completely open, and a cleaned excerpt is bundled directly
        into R as the <c>gapminder</c> package: 1,704 observations covering 142
        countries from 1952 to 2007, with one row per country per year and
        columns for <c>country</c>, <c>continent</c>, <c>year</c>,
        <c>lifeExp</c> (life expectancy at birth, in years), <c>pop</c>
        (population), and <c>gdpPercap</c> (GDP per capita, in
        inflation-adjusted US dollars).
      </p>

      <p>
        A single row tells you almost nothing interesting.
        But, you can build up this data nicely, one variable ("dimension") at a
        time, into a single animated chart.
        We will do the same thing here — starting from a blank canvas and adding
        one dimension at a time.
      </p>

      <p>
        First, install and load the packages:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # Note that you may not need to run these install.packages lines if you already have them installed.
        install.packages("gapminder")
        install.packages("ggplot2")
        install.packages("dplyr")

        library(gapminder)
        library(ggplot2)
        library(dplyr)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Before plotting, it is worth taking a moment to understand what the
        dataset actually contains.
        In R, <c>?gapminder</c> opens the documentation for the package:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
    ?gapminder
        </code>
      </program>
      <pre>
    Excerpt of the Gapminder data on life expectancy, GDP per capita,
    and population by country.

    Format
    The main data frame gapminder has 1704 rows and 6 variables:

    country     factor with 142 levels
    continent   factor with 5 levels
    year        ranges from 1952 to 2007 in increments of 5 years
    lifeExp     life expectancy at birth, in years
    pop         population
    gdpPercap   GDP per capita (US$, inflation-adjusted)
      </pre>
      <p>
        A few things worth noting before we start.
        <c>country</c> and <c>continent</c> are both factors.
        These are categorical variables with a fixed set of levels, discussed
        further in <xref ref="ch-handling-factors"/>.
        <c>year</c> is not continuous: it jumps in five-year steps, so there is
        1952, 1957, 1962, and so on.
        <c>gdpPercap</c> is inflation-adjusted, meaning values across different
        years have been made comparable in real terms.
        And <c>pop</c> is a raw count, which, as we will see, varies so
        enormously between countries that it needs careful handling to show
        clearly in a plot.
      </p>

      <p>
        We will start by working with just the year as 2007.
        To do this, we simply filter our data set
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        gapminder_2007 &lt;- gapminder_2007 %&gt;%
              dplyr::filter(year == 2007)

        dim(gapminder_2007)
        #&gt; [1] 142   6

        head(gapminder_2007)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 6
        #&gt;   country     continent  year lifeExp      pop gdpPercap
        #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;       &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Afghanistan Asia       2007    43.8 31889923      975.
        #&gt; 2 Albania     Europe     2007    76.4  3600523     5937.
        #&gt; 3 Algeria     Africa     2007    72.3 33333216     6223.
        #&gt; 4 Angola      Africa     2007    42.7 12420476     4797.
        #&gt; 5 Argentina   Americas   2007    75.3 40301927    12779.
        #&gt; 6 Australia   Oceania    2007    81.2 20434176    34435.
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        We can see there are six variables, and there is one country per row.
        The first row shows Afghanistan in 2007 had a life expectancy of 43.8
        years, population 31 million, and GDP per capita $975.
        This tells us something, but not much.
        We could instead look at how all 142 countries (rows) compare to one
        another.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-2d">
      <title>Two dimensions: does money buy a longer life?</title>

      <p>
        Start with the most basic possible question: is a country's income
        related to how long its people live?
        <c>ggplot2</c> uses a system called the "Grammar of Graphics", where the
        plot happens in layers.
        The first layer is always the same: you tell R which dataset to use, and
        which variables to map onto which visual properties (the "
        <term>aesthetic mappings</term> ").
        Here, we map <c>gdpPercap</c> onto the x-axis and <c>lifeExp</c> onto
        the y-axis.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp))
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-2d-empty">
        <caption>
          A blank plot, with <c>gdpPercap</c> on the x axis and <c>lifeExp</c>
          on the y axis.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_2d_empty.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A blank rectangle, with <c>gdpPercap</c> on the x axis and
              <c>lifeExp</c> on the y axis.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This gives you a blank canvas with the axes set up but nothing drawn on
        it yet.
        To actually draw something, you add a <term>geom</term>.
        This is short for geometric object, which is the shape used to represent
        each observation.
        <c>geom_point()</c> draws one point per row, giving us a scatterplot.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp)) +
          geom_point()
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-2d">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita against life expectancy for 142 countries in 2007.
          The relationship is positive but curved — richer countries tend to
          have longer life expectancies, but with diminishing returns at higher
          incomes.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_2d.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatterplot with GDP per capita on the x-axis and life
              expectancy in years on the y-axis.
              Points are clustered toward the lower left for poorer countries
              with lower life expectancies, and spread toward the upper right
              for wealthier countries.
              The relationship is curved rather than linear, with a steep rise
              at low income levels that flattens out at higher incomes.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        There is a clear positive relationship, but the x-axis is awkward.
        A handful of very wealthy countries are spread far to the right,
        compressing the rest of the points into a dense cluster on the left.
        This is a right-skewed distribution: most countries are bunched at lower
        incomes, with a long tail of a few rich ones.
        A log scale fixes this by spreading out the lower end and compressing
        the upper end, making the pattern across the full range much easier to
        read.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can also add in a further layer <c>labs</c> which relabels our axes
        to something more intuitive than the default variable names.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-2d-log">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries
          in 2007.
          The log scale spreads the x-axis more evenly, making the positive
          relationship across all income levels clearer.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_2d_log.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatterplot with GDP per capita on a log scale on the x-axis and
              life expectancy in years on the y-axis.
              The points now spread more evenly across the x-axis, and a roughly
              linear positive relationship is visible across the full range.
              Axis labels read GDP per capita (log scale) and Life expectancy.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        There are two dimensions because we have two variables, and one clear
        trend.
        But every point is currently the same colour and the same size — a
        Norwegian point and a Kenyan point are visually identical except for
        their position.
        We have used the x-axis and y-axis, and that is all.
        There is more in the data for us to use to make this plot clearer, and
        to explore the relationships further.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-3d">
      <title>Three dimensions: adding continent</title>

      <p>
        We can add a third variable without adding a new axis.
        Instead of position, we use colour.
        Mapping <c>continent</c> onto <c>colour</c> assigns a different colour
        to each continent's points.
        This is added into <c>aes</c> as it is a new dimension.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp, 
                                   colour = continent)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-3d">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries
          in 2007, coloured by continent.
          African countries cluster toward the lower left; European and Oceanian
          countries toward the upper right.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_3d.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatterplot with the same axes as before.
              Points are now coloured by continent — African points (red)
              cluster toward the lower left of the chart, European and Oceanian
              points cluster toward the upper right, and American and Asian
              points are spread across the middle.
              A colour legend identifies each continent.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The overall trend from before is still there, but now it has structure.
        African countries cluster toward the lower left, European countries
        toward the upper right, and the spread within each continent becomes
        visible for the first time.
        Nothing about the underlying data changed.
        One aesthetic mapping did.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is also worth considering accessibility.
        Colour alone can be difficult to distinguish for readers who are
        colourblind.
        Adding <c>shape = continent</c> alongside <c>colour = continent</c>
        means each continent is distinguished by both colour and point shape,
        making the chart readable even when printed in black and white or viewed
        by someone with colour blindness.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp, 
                                   colour = continent,
                                   shape = continent)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-3d-2">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries
          in 2007.
          Each point has it's own colour and shape per continent.
          African countries cluster toward the lower left; European and Oceanian
          countries toward the upper right.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_3d_2.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatterplot with the same axes as before.
              Points are now coloured by continent — African points (red,
              circle) cluster toward the lower left of the chart, European and
              Oceanian points cluster toward the upper right, and American and
              Asian points are spread across the middle.
              A colour and shape legend identifies each continent.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Note that <c>colour</c> and <c>shape</c> are both mapped to the same
        variable, <c>continent</c>, so they are not adding a new dimension, just
        making the third dimension more robust.
        We will keep both from here on.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-4d">
      <title>Four dimensions: adding population</title>

      <p>
        A fourth variable can be added using point size.
        Mapping <c>pop</c> onto <c>size</c> makes each point's area proportional
        to the country's population, turning the scatterplot into a bubble
        chart.
        We also add <c>alpha = 0.7</c> inside <c>geom_point()</c>.
        <c>alpha</c> controls the transparency of the points, where 1 is fully
        opaque and 0 is invisible.
        At 0.7, overlapping points remain visible through each other, which
        helps when large bubbles sit in front of smaller ones.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                                   colour = continent, shape = continent,
                                   size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 15)) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-4d">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries
          in 2007, with point size proportional to population and colour and
          shape indicating continent.
          China and India dominate by size; Kenya sits in the African cluster
          toward the lower left.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_4d.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bubble chart with the same axes and colour/shape encoding as
              before.
              Point sizes now vary substantially — two very large points (China
              and India) are visible in the Asian cluster in the middle of the
              chart.
              Most other points are considerably smaller.
              A size legend shows population scale.
              African points remain clustered toward the lower left.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        China and India, similar in income and life expectancy to several
        smaller countries, now visibly dominate by sheer size.
        But where is Kenya?
        The African cluster is dense and it is hard to identify individual
        countries.
        We can add labels for specific countries of interest, and a good general
        technique for drawing attention to particular observations without
        labelling everything.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(dplyr)

        # Countries to label
        label_countries &lt;- c("Kenya", "Nigeria", "South Africa", 
                              "Ethiopia", "China", "India")

        gapminder_labels &lt;- gapminder_2007 %&gt;%
          filter(country %in% label_countries)

        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                                   colour = continent, shape = continent,
                                   size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          geom_text(data = gapminder_labels, 
                    aes(label = country), 
                    size = 3, nudge_y = 1, show.legend = FALSE) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-4d-labels">
        <caption>
          The same chart as above with selected countries labelled, including
          Kenya, Nigeria, South Africa, Ethiopia, China, and India.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_4d_labels.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bubble chart as before, with text labels added for Kenya,
              Nigeria, South Africa, Ethiopia, China, and India.
              Kenya appears in the African cluster toward the lower left of the
              chart.
              China and India are labelled near the large bubbles in the Asian
              cluster.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-5d">
      <title>Five dimensions: adding time</title>

      <p>
        The chart so far is a single snapshot displaying 2007.
        Rosling's original animated the chart across every year in the data,
        letting countries move as their income and life expectancy changed.
        We can show the same fifth dimension, time, as small multiples using
        <c>facet_wrap()</c>, which produces one panel per year and lays them out
        in a grid.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                              colour = continent, shape = continent,
                              size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
          facet_wrap(~ year) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-5d">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries,
          faceted by year from 1952 to 2007.
          Read left to right, top to bottom: life expectancy climbs steadily in
          almost every country, even where income barely moves.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_5d.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A grid of 12 small scatterplots, one every 5 years from 1952 to
              2007, each with the same axes, colour, shape, and size encoding as
              the 2007 chart.
              Across the panels, the overall cloud of points shifts upward and
              to the right, showing rising life expectancy and income across
              most countries over time.
              A few panels show individual bubbles that drop backward, visible
              as anomalies against the general upward trend.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Read left to right, top to bottom, and entire countries visibly migrate
        across the panels: life expectancy climbing steadily almost everywhere,
        even where income barely moves.
        There is the occasional bubble dropping backward in a single panel,
        indicating a sharp reversal.
        This is worth investigating rather than dismissing as noise.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can also adjust the visual style of the whole chart using a
        <term>theme</term>.
        Themes control the background, gridlines, fonts, and other non-data
        elements.
        <c>theme_bw()</c> gives a clean white background with grey gridlines,
        which many people find easier to read than the default grey background.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                              colour = continent, shape = continent,
                              size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
          facet_wrap(~ year) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population") +
          theme_bw()
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        If you want a true animation, you can get countries moving smoothly
        rather than jumping between panels using the <c>gganimate</c> package.
        This adds that with one extra line.
        We also add in <c>labs(title = "Year: {frame_time}")</c> which just
        simply means that the year that is being shown in the animation is
        visible.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # install.packages("gganimate")
        library(gganimate)
        library(dplyr)
ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = gdpPercap, 
                      y = lifeExp,
                      colour = continent, 
                      shape = continent,
                      size = pop,
                      group = country)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
  labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", 
       y = "Life expectancy",
       size = "Population") + 
      transition_time(year) +
    labs(title = "Year: {frame_time}")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapminder-5d-animate">
        <caption>
          GDP per capita (log scale) against life expectancy for 142 countries,
          animated by year from 1952 to 2007.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch5_gapminder_animate.gif" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A gif animating the 12 small scatterplots from the previous plot
              so that the previous plot transitions from one time point to the
              next seamlessly.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <insight>
        <p>
          Watch the animation carefully.
          In 1977, one country's bubble drops sharply downward — a sudden fall
          in life expectancy that stands out against the general upward trend.
          And in 1992, it happens again, even more dramatically.
          Before assuming these are errors in the data, it is worth remembering
          what we discussed in <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>: a sudden
          implausible-looking value is not always wrong.
          Sometimes it is the data telling you something real.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can find out which countries these are by filtering the data to
          look for unusually low life expectancies in those years:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    library(dplyr)

    # Which country had the lowest life expectancy in 1977?
    gapminder %&gt;%
      filter(year == 1977) %&gt;%
      arrange(lifeExp) %&gt;%
      head(5)
    #&gt; # A tibble: 5 x 6
    #&gt;   country      continent  year lifeExp      pop gdpPercap
    #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;        &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
    #&gt; 1 Cambodia      Asia       1977    31.2  6978607      525.
    #&gt; 2 Sierra Leone  Africa     1977    36.8  3140897     1348.
    #&gt; 3 Guinea-Bissau Africa     1977    37.5   745228      765.
    #&gt; 4 Afghanistan   Asia       1977    38.4 14880372      786.
    #&gt; 5 Angola        Africa     1977    39.5  6162675     3009.

    # Which country had the lowest life expectancy in 1992?
    gapminder %&gt;%
      filter(year == 1992) %&gt;%
      arrange(lifeExp) %&gt;%
      head(5)
    #&gt; # A tibble: 5 x 6
    #&gt;   country      continent  year lifeExp      pop gdpPercap
    #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;        &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
    #&gt; 1 Rwanda       Africa     1992    23.6  7290203      737.
    #&gt; 2 Sierra Leone Africa     1992    38.3  4260884     1069.
    #&gt; 3 Somalia      Africa     1992    39.7  6099799      927.
    #&gt; 4 Angola       Africa     1992    40.6  8735988     2628.
    #&gt; 5 Liberia      Africa     1992    40.8  1912974      637.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The 1977 drop is Cambodia, during the Khmer Rouge genocide which ran
          from 1975 to 1979.
          The 1992 drop is Rwanda, in the years surrounding the Rwandan genocide
          of 1994.
          Neither is a data error.
          Both are real events, visible in the data as a sudden reversal against
          a trend that was moving the other way everywhere else.
          This is one of the things that multidimensional visualisation makes
          possible: an anomaly that might be buried in a table of 1,704 rows
          becomes immediately visible when you can see all countries moving
          together over time.
          The next question of if this anomoly is a mistake or something real is
          exactly the right question to ask.
          Here, the answer is history, and a quick search can explain it.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-summary">
      <title>What just happened</title>

      <p>
        Five variables, five aesthetic mappings, all in one graphic.
        Each variable was added to the <em>same</em> visualisation, using a
        different visual property to encode it.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-gapminder-dimensions">
        <title>Dimensions added to the Gapminder chart, one at a time</title>

        <tabular halign="left">
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Dimension</cell>
            <cell>Aesthetic</cell>
            <cell>Variable</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>1st &amp; 2nd</cell>
            <cell>x, y position</cell>
            <cell><c>gdpPercap</c>, <c>lifeExp</c></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>3rd</cell>
            <cell>colour &amp; shape</cell>
            <cell><c>continent</c></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>4th</cell>
            <cell>size</cell>
            <cell><c>pop</c></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>5th</cell>
            <cell>facet / animation frame</cell>
            <cell><c>year</c></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Each new variable was added as a new <term>aesthetic mapping</term>.
        Position, colour, shape, size, and facet are all different ways of
        encoding information in the same image.
        The elephant data earlier in the chapter was the same idea: GPS
        location, water availability, human modification, sex, and time, held
        together in one analysis rather than one table per variable.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Bryan, J.
        <c>gapminder</c> R package, data curated by the Gapminder Foundation (
        <url href="https://www.gapminder.org" visual="gapminder.org"/> ), based
        on the work of Hans Rosling.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-visualising-multidimensional">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-elephants-variables">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-following-the-giants"/> pairs each elephant's GPS
          location with several other variables: vegetation cover, distance to
          water, human infrastructure, sex, season, and time of day.
          It describes findings that came from examining location jointly with
          land cover, protection status, sex and season.
          Pick one variable not explicitly named in that combination <mdash/>
          time of day, or distance to water <mdash/> and describe, in a sentence
          or two, what mapping it onto colour, size, or facet might reveal that
          the described analysis would not.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-flamingo-dimensions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> from
          <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>.
          Build a single plot that encodes at least four variables at once (for
          example: bill length and body mass on the axes, species on colour,
          lake on facet).
          What does the combined view show that a single-variable summary of
          each column, taken separately, would not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-gapminder-label">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Adapt the code in <xref ref="ss-gapminder-5d"/> to add a label for a
          country of your choice into the animation.
          How does it change over the course of the fifty-five years?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-gapminder-1952">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Adapt the code in <xref ref="ss-gapminder-4d"/> to show 1952 instead
          of 2007.
          Compare the two charts.
          Which continents have moved the most in the fifty-five years between
          them, and along which axis?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-too-many-dimensions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-gapminder-5d"/> uses position, colour, size, and facet
          together, four aesthetics beyond the two axes.
          Is there a point at which adding another variable to the same chart
          would make it harder to read rather than easier?
          Argue for a specific upper limit, and say what you would do with a
          fifth or sixth variable instead of cramming it into the same plot.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>